.TH remount 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
remount \- change a mounted filesystem between read-only and read-write
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B remount
[\fB\-n\fR] \fIdevice\fR|\fIdirectory\fR \fBro\fR|\fBrw\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B remount
changes a filesystem that is already mounted, without unmounting it.
The filesystem may be named by device or by mount point;
.B remount
looks it up in
.B /etc/fstab
and then in
.BR /etc/mtab ,
and failing both takes the name as given.
.PP
This is the only way to make the
.I root
safe, since the root cannot be unmounted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Do not update
.BR /etc/mtab .
Needed when
.B /etc
is on the filesystem being made read-only, and what
.BR reflash.py
uses before resetting the board.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B ro
Read-only.  Buffers are flushed first.
.TP
.B rw
Read-write again.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# remount -n / ro          before flashing a kernel
# remount / rw
.fi
.SH NOTES
Making the root read-only before a reset is what saves the next boot
from a long
.BR fsck :
.B picoctl flash
resets immediately and unmounts nothing on the way out, so a root left
read-write comes back dirty.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (1),
.BR umount (1),
.BR sync (1),
.BR fsck (1),
.BR picoctl (1)
